Lansing, MI 48824 USA (e-mail: vahidimo@msu.edu, zhukeyi1@msu.edu, zhangk64@msu.edu,
and lizhaoj1@egr.msu.edu).</span></span></span><span id="id5" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Ziyou Song is with the Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, University of Michigan, Ann Arbor, MI 48109 USA (e-mail: ziyou@umich.edu).</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Data-enabled predictive control (DeePC) has recently emerged as a powerful data-driven approach for efficient system controls with constraints handling capabilities. It performs optimal controls by directly harnessing input-output (I/O) data, bypassing the process of explicit model identification that can be costly and time-consuming. However, its high computational complexity, driven by a large-scale optimization problem (typically in a higher dimension than its model-based counterpart–Model Predictive Control), hinders real-time applications. To overcome this limitation, we propose the data-enabled neighboring extremal (DeeNE) framework, which significantly reduces computational cost while preserving control performance. DeeNE leverages first-order optimality perturbation analysis to efficiently update a precomputed nominal DeePC solution in response to changes in initial conditions and reference trajectories. We validate its effectiveness on a 7-DoF KINOVA Gen3 robotic arm, demonstrating substantial computational savings and robust, data-driven control performance.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>Nonlinear Optimal Control, Data-Enabled Predictive Control, Numerical Optimization Algorithm, Robotic Arms.

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Safe and high-precision trajectory tracking for autonomous systems has traditionally relied on model-based control synthesis, which depends on accurate dynamical models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>.
Model predictive control (MPC), for instance, has been widely successful in ensuring both tracking performance and system safety across various applications. However, its reliance on precise models and substantial onboard computational resources limits its broader adoption in autonomous systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. This challenge is particularly pronounced for compliant or low-cost robots/vehicles performing high-precision tasks or operating in rapidly changing environments, such as construction sites where robots/vehicles are exposed to severe weather and working conditions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.
To mitigate computational complexity, researchers have explored control policy learning approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> and reduced-order modeling techniques <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, though these methods often require extensive data collection or involve trade-offs between system performance and computational efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, data-driven optimal controllers have gained considerable attention in both academia and industry, as they eliminate the requirement for an explicit system model, which can be costly and time-consuming to develop. For example, in robot manipulation, deep learning techniques have been applied to learn robot kinematics, including both forward and inverse kinematics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. Reinforcement learning and iterative learning control are also prominent methods for learning-based control in robotic manipulators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. Gaussian Process Regression for robotics tasks has been outlined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> and extended to MPC for robotic manipulators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. To overcome the need for explicit models and reduce computational time, spatial-temporal filters have been used for MPC policy learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. Recently, a new family of neural networks – neural ordinary differential equations (NODE) - has emerged as an effective tool for extracting dynamic models from data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, as it approximates a continuous-depth neural network to directly model differential equations. This algorithm has been extended to a NODE-based MPC framework for aerial robots <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. However, while data-driven controllers offer significant advantages, they often require extensive training data to achieve reliable control performance, and their lack of interpretability remains a key challenge.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Data-enabled predictive control (DeePC) has recently emerged as a new powerful data-driven optimal control approach, transitioning from traditional MPC to a model-free framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. By leveraging raw input/output (I/O) data, DeePC directly seeks an optimal control policy without requiring prior system identification. Building on the Fundamental Lemma <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> and inspired by behavioral system theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, DeePC offers a novel approach to learning, predicting, and controlling system behavior by representing the subspace of the I/O trajectories as the column span of a data Hankel matrix. Regularization techniques and slack variables can be incorporated to address overfitting and infeasibility issues <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, which can arise from noisy data and nonlinear systems. In comparison with the aforementioned machine learning-based controllers, DeePC is less data hungry <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. More importantly, DeePC can explicitly handle constraints, which are crucial in many safety-critical real-world engineering systems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Despite its promise, DeePC often suffers from high computational complexity due to the large dimensionality of the optimization variable, which is generally higher than that of its model-based MPC counterpart <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. To address this challenge, this paper introduces a computationally efficient data-driven control framework, data-enabled neighboring extremal (DeeNE), which significantly enhances the computational efficiency of DeePC with minimum or no degradation in control performance. First-order optimality perturbation analysis on a nominal DeePC solution is conducted to derive a feedback correction law that adapts to I/O perturbations. Compared to our previous conference version <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, this work extends the DeeNE framework to incorporate real-time reference trajectory perturbations, enabling adaptive updates to the control policy for varying desired trajectories. Furthermore, the framework is validated through both simulation and experimental studies on a 7-DoF KINOVA Gen3 robotic arm, demonstrating substantial improvements in computational efficiency and constraint-handling capabilities in real-world applications.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The remainder of the paper is organized as follows: Section II introduces the problem formulation and reviews the DeePC basics. Section III presents the main results on the DeeNE framework. Simulation and experimental results are shown in Section IV. Finally, Section V concludes the paper.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Notations</span>. We adopt the following notations across the paper. <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n\times m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n\times m}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the set of <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional real vectors and the set of <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times m</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional real matrices, respectively. <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> stand for the transpose of the vector <math id="S1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and the matrix <math id="S1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. <math id="S1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lVert\cdot\rVert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lVert\cdot\rVert</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Euclidean norm of a vector or the induced <math id="S1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-norm of a matrix. <math id="S1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> stands for the identity matrix with <math id="S1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimension. <math id="S1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{gg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{gg}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{gr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{gr}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{rg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{rg}</annotation></semantics></math> represent <math id="S1.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial J}{\partial g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial J}{\partial g}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial^{2}J}{\partial g^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>J</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial^{2}J}{\partial g^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial^{2}J}{\partial g\partial r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>J</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial^{2}J}{\partial g\partial r}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial^{2}J}{\partial r\partial g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>J</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial^{2}J}{\partial r\partial g}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Problem Formulation and Preliminaries</span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce the problem of reference tracking for nonlinear systems with constraints, followed by the model-based optimal control formulation, which is called model predictive control (MPC). We then present the data-driven predictive control formulation that bypasses the dynamic modeling process, which is called data-enabled predictive control (DeePC).</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Model-Based Reference Tracking</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Consider the following discrete-time nonlinear system:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E1X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x(k+1)=f(x(k),u(k))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x(k+1)=f(x(k),u(k)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E1Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y(k)=h(x(k),u(k))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y(k)=h(x(k),u(k)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathbb{N}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathbb{N}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the time step, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the state vector of the system, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in{\mathbb{R}^{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in{\mathbb{R}^{m}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the control input, and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathbb{R}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathbb{R}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the outputs of the system. Moreover, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}^{n}\times\mathbb{R}^{m}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}^{n}\times\mathbb{R}^{m}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the system dynamics with <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(0,0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(0,0)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:\mathbb{R}^{n}\times\mathbb{R}^{m}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:\mathbb{R}^{n}\times\mathbb{R}^{m}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the output dynamics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We consider the following safety constraint:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(y(k),u(k))\leq 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(y(k),u(k))\leq 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C:\mathbb{R}^{p}\times\mathbb{R}^{m}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>l</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C:\mathbb{R}^{p}\times\mathbb{R}^{m}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{l}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> denoting the total number of constraints in inputs and outputs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">We consider a tracking control problem for the nonlinear system (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In II-A Model-Based Reference Tracking ‣ II Problem Formulation and Preliminaries ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) with a desired output reference trajectory <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(k),k=0,1,2,\cdots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋯</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(k),k=0,1,2,\cdots</annotation></semantics></math>. Such a problem can be solved using a receding horizon optimal control, also known as model predictive control (MPC) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, by minimizing the following cost term over a prescribed horizon of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> steps:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E3X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle J_{N}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u};\mathbf{r})=\sum^{N-1}_{k=0}\phi(y(k),u(k);r(k))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle J_{N}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u};\mathbf{r})=\sum^{N-1}_{k=0}\phi(y(k),u(k);r(k)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}=[u(0),\,u(1),\,\cdots,\,u(N-1)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.059em">,</mo><mo rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}=[u(0),\,u(1),\,\cdots,\,u(N-1)]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}=[y(0),\,y(1),\,\cdots,\,y(N-1)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.059em">,</mo><mo rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}=[y(0),\,y(1),\,\cdots,\,y(N-1)]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}=\left[r(0),\,r(1),\,\cdots,\,r(N-1)\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.059em">,</mo><mo rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}=\left[r(0),\,r(1),\,\cdots,\,r(N-1)\right]</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(y,u;r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(y,u;r)</annotation></semantics></math> is the stage cost, which generally includes the tracking error and the control efforts.
Therefore, with an initial state <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{o}</annotation></semantics></math>, the optimal tracking problem over <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> steps can be reduced to the following constrained optimization problem:</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E4X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\mathbf{y}^{*},\mathbf{u}^{*})=\underset{\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u}}{\arg\min}\hskip 2.84526ptJ_{N}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u};\mathbf{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐲</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐮</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\mathbf{y}^{*},\mathbf{u}^{*})=\underset{\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u}}{\arg\min}\hskip 2.84526ptJ_{N}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u};\mathbf{r})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="4" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E4Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s.t.\hskip 14.22636ptx(k+1)=f(x(k),u(k))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo><mspace style="width:1.587em;" width="1.587em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s.t.\hskip 14.22636ptx(k+1)=f(x(k),u(k)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E4Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y(k)=h(x(k),u(k))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y(k)=h(x(k),u(k)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E4Xc" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4Xc.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle C(y(k),u(k))\leq 0,\quad x(0)=x_{o}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C(y(k),u(k))\leq 0,\quad x(0)=x_{o}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In the MPC setting, only the first optimal control (i.e., <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(0)</annotation></semantics></math>) is executed, and the system evolves one step. The process is then repeated with the current state of the system as the new initial state for MPC.
The above MPC formulation (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In II-A Model-Based Reference Tracking ‣ II Problem Formulation and Preliminaries ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) has been a celebrated and widely used control technique for trajectory tracking, due to its capability to enforce safety constraints during the control design. The key ingredient for this controller is an accurate parametric model of the system, but obtaining such a model, using plant modeling or identification procedures, is often the most time-consuming and costly part of control design. We next introduce its data-driven counterpart, DeePC, which is able to enforce safety constraints on the control design and performs similar predictive control without the need for an explicit dynamical model.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Data-Enabled Predictive Control (DeePC)</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">DeePC has recently emerged as a popular data-driven optimal control framework that has achieved numerous successes. It directly harnesses the raw input and output data for controls, eliminating the requirement of an explicit dynamic model as used in (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In II-A Model-Based Reference Tracking ‣ II Problem Formulation and Preliminaries ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). Building on the Fundamental Lemma <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, DeePC leverages a non-parametric representation of the dynamic system following the behavioral system theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. Specifically, Hankel matrices <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}_{T_{ini}+N}(u^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}_{T_{ini}+N}(u^{d})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}_{T_{ini}+N}(y^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}_{T_{ini}+N}(y^{d})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{ini}+N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{ini}+N</annotation></semantics></math> represents the depth of the Hankel matrix, are first constructed from the offline collected input/output (I/O) samples <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E5X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{H}_{T_{ini}+N}(u^{d})=\begin{bmatrix}u^{d}_{1}&amp;u^{d}_{2}&amp;\cdots&amp;u^{d}_{T-T_{ini}-N+1}\\
u^{d}_{2}&amp;u^{d}_{3}&amp;\cdots&amp;u^{d}_{T-T_{ini}-N+2}\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
u^{d}_{T_{ini}+N}&amp;u^{d}_{T_{ini}+N+1}&amp;\cdots&amp;u^{d}_{T}\end{bmatrix}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ℍ</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">N</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">u</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msup><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">u</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">u</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mo mathsize="0.900em">⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">N</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">u</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">u</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">3</mn><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mo mathsize="0.900em">⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">N</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">u</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">N</mi></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">u</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">N</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mo mathsize="0.900em">⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">u</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{H}_{T_{ini}+N}(u^{d})=\begin{bmatrix}u^{d}_{1}&amp;u^{d}_{2}&amp;\cdots&amp;u^{d}_{T-T_{ini}-N+1}\\
u^{d}_{2}&amp;u^{d}_{3}&amp;\cdots&amp;u^{d}_{T-T_{ini}-N+2}\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
u^{d}_{T_{ini}+N}&amp;u^{d}_{T_{ini}+N+1}&amp;\cdots&amp;u^{d}_{T}\end{bmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> denote the length of the initial trajectory, prediction trajectory, and collected data, respectively. <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}_{T_{ini}+N}(u^{d})\in\mathbb{R}^{m(T_{ini}+N)\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}_{T_{ini}+N}(u^{d})\in\mathbb{R}^{m(T_{ini}+N)\times L}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=T-T_{ini}-N+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=T-T_{ini}-N+1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}_{T_{ini}+N}(y^{d})\in\mathbb{R}^{p(T_{ini}+N)\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}_{T_{ini}+N}(y^{d})\in\mathbb{R}^{p(T_{ini}+N)\times L}</annotation></semantics></math> is constructed in an analogous way from the collected samples <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. It should be mentioned that persistency of excitation requirement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> is generally needed for the signal <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be met if <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}_{T_{ini}+N+n}(u^{d})\in\mathbb{R}^{m(T_{ini}+N+n)\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}_{T_{ini}+N+n}(u^{d})\in\mathbb{R}^{m(T_{ini}+N+n)\times L}</annotation></semantics></math> has full row rank.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">One can partition the Hankel matrices into <span id="S2.SS2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">past</span> and <span id="S2.SS2.p2.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">future</span> sub-blocks as:</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E6X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{bmatrix}U_{P}\\
U_{F}\end{bmatrix}=:\mathbb{H}_{T_{ini}+N}(u^{d}),\hskip 14.22636pt\begin{bmatrix}Y_{P}\\
Y_{F}\end{bmatrix}=:\mathbb{H}_{T_{ini}+N}(y^{d})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>ℍ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="1.587em">,</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>ℍ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{bmatrix}U_{P}\\
U_{F}\end{bmatrix}=:\mathbb{H}_{T_{ini}+N}(u^{d}),\hskip 14.22636pt\begin{bmatrix}Y_{P}\\
Y_{F}\end{bmatrix}=:\mathbb{H}_{T_{ini}+N}(y^{d}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{P}\in\mathbb{R}^{mT_{ini}\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{P}\in\mathbb{R}^{mT_{ini}\times L}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{F}\in\mathbb{R}^{mN\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{F}\in\mathbb{R}^{mN\times L}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{P}\in\mathbb{R}^{pT_{ini}\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{P}\in\mathbb{R}^{pT_{ini}\times L}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{F}\in\mathbb{R}^{pN\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{F}\in\mathbb{R}^{pN\times L}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, DeePC aims at optimizing the system performance over <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> future steps using only the I/O data (<a href="#S2.E6" title="In II-B Data-Enabled Predictive Control (DeePC) ‣ II Problem Formulation and Preliminaries ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), which is presented as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E7X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\mathbf{y}^{*},\mathbf{u}^{*},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}^{*},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}}^{*},\mathbf{g}^{*})=\underset{\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{g}}{\arg\min}\hskip 2.84526ptJ_{N}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{g},\mathbf{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></mmultiscripts><mo>,</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></mmultiscripts><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐲</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐮</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">σ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝐲</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">σ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝐮</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐠</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐮</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\mathbf{y}^{*},\mathbf{u}^{*},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}^{*},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}}^{*},\mathbf{g}^{*})=\underset{\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{g}}{\arg\min}\hskip 2.84526ptJ_{N}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{g},\mathbf{r})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E7Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s.t.\hskip 14.22636pt\begin{bmatrix}U_{P}\\
Y_{P}\\
U_{F}\\
Y_{F}\end{bmatrix}g=\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}\\
y_{ini}\\
u\\
y\end{bmatrix}+\begin{bmatrix}\sigma_{u}\\
\sigma_{y}\\
0\\
0\end{bmatrix}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo><mspace style="width:1.587em;" width="1.587em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>u</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>y</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s.t.\hskip 14.22636pt\begin{bmatrix}U_{P}\\
Y_{P}\\
U_{F}\\
Y_{F}\end{bmatrix}g=\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}\\
y_{ini}\\
u\\
y\end{bmatrix}+\begin{bmatrix}\sigma_{u}\\
\sigma_{y}\\
0\\
0\end{bmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E7Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle C(y,u)\leq 0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C(y,u)\leq 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.5" class="ltx_p">where the equality constraint is a result of Fundamental lemma with <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{u}\in\mathbb{R}^{mT_{ini}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{u}\in\mathbb{R}^{mT_{ini}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{y}\in\mathbb{R}^{pT_{ini}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{y}\in\mathbb{R}^{pT_{ini}}</annotation></semantics></math> being auxiliary slack variables to model measurement noises and nonlinearities, and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u_{ini},y_{ini})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u_{ini},y_{ini})</annotation></semantics></math> is the given initial trajectory. Moreover, <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{N}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{g},r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐮</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{N}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{g},r)</annotation></semantics></math> is the modified cost function including two penalty terms for slack variables and also a regularization term to avoid overfitting caused by noisy data and/or system nonlinearity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Using the identities <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=Y_{F}g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=Y_{F}g</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=U_{F}g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=U_{F}g</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{y}=Y_{P}g-y_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{y}=Y_{P}g-y_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{u}=U_{P}g-u_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{u}=U_{P}g-u_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math>, one can rewrite (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In II-B Data-Enabled Predictive Control (DeePC) ‣ II Problem Formulation and Preliminaries ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) as:</p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E8X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{g}^{*}=\underset{\mathbf{g}}{\arg\min}\hskip 2.84526ptJ_{N}(Y_{F}\mathbf{g},U_{F}\mathbf{g},Y_{P}\mathbf{g}-y_{ini},U_{P}\mathbf{g}-u_{ini},\mathbf{g},\mathbf{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝐠</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐠</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐠</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐠</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐠</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{g}^{*}=\underset{\mathbf{g}}{\arg\min}\hskip 2.84526ptJ_{N}(Y_{F}\mathbf{g},U_{F}\mathbf{g},Y_{P}\mathbf{g}-y_{ini},U_{P}\mathbf{g}-u_{ini},\mathbf{g},\mathbf{r})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E8Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s.t.\hskip 14.22636ptC(Y_{F}g,U_{F}g)\leq 0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo><mspace style="width:1.587em;" width="1.587em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s.t.\hskip 14.22636ptC(Y_{F}g,U_{F}g)\leq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">If the constraint <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(y,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(y,u)</annotation></semantics></math> was absent in (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In II-B Data-Enabled Predictive Control (DeePC) ‣ II Problem Formulation and Preliminaries ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), the problem is referred to the unconstrained DeePC, and the solution is available in closed form <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=U_{F}g=K_{d}^{r}r+K_{d}^{ini}w_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=U_{F}g=K_{d}^{r}r+K_{d}^{ini}w_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{d}^{r}\in\mathbb{R}^{mN\times pN}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{d}^{r}\in\mathbb{R}^{mN\times pN}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{d}^{ini}\in\mathbb{R}^{mN\times(m+p)T_{ini}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{d}^{ini}\in\mathbb{R}^{mN\times(m+p)T_{ini}}</annotation></semantics></math> are control gains, and <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{ini}=\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}^{T},y_{ini}^{T}\end{bmatrix}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{ini}=\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}^{T},y_{ini}^{T}\end{bmatrix}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> are the given desired reference trajectory and initial trajectory. However, in the general case with system constraints, the DeePC-induced optimization (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In II-B Data-Enabled Predictive Control (DeePC) ‣ II Problem Formulation and Preliminaries ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) generally suffers from high computational time since the dimension of the optimization variable <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is exceedingly high to ensure the persistency of excitation requirement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. In the next section, we present a computationally efficient DeePC framework to mitigate the computation complexity.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE)</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, inspired by neighboring extremal framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, we propose a new DeePC framework, named data-enabled neighboring extremal (DeeNE), to significantly reduce computational cost with no or little performance degradation. Specifically, consider the nominal solution <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g^{o},u^{o},y^{o})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g^{o},u^{o},y^{o})</annotation></semantics></math> under the given initial I/O trajectory <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{o}_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>o</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{o}_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math> and reference trajectory <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{o}</annotation></semantics></math> in the DeePC formulation (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In II-B Data-Enabled Predictive Control (DeePC) ‣ II Problem Formulation and Preliminaries ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>). Now for a new initial I/O trajectory <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math> and/or reference trajectory <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, instead of rerunning the computationally expensive optimization in  (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In II-B Data-Enabled Predictive Control (DeePC) ‣ II Problem Formulation and Preliminaries ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), we seek an optimal feedback policy for correcting the DeePC solution as <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{*}=u^{o}+\delta u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{*}=u^{o}+\delta u</annotation></semantics></math> using perturbation analysis. The objective is now to present the derivation of the feedback gains, corresponding to the perturbations <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta w_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta w_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta r</annotation></semantics></math>, for the optimal correction policy <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta u</annotation></semantics></math>. We will first consider the case of the nominal trajectory obtained by solving  (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In II-B Data-Enabled Predictive Control (DeePC) ‣ II Problem Formulation and Preliminaries ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) (i.e., optimal nominal trajectory) and then consider the case of a non-optimal nominal trajectory.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">DeeNE with Optimal Nominal Solution</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Following (<a href="#S2.E8" title="In II-B Data-Enabled Predictive Control (DeePC) ‣ II Problem Formulation and Preliminaries ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), we first construct the following augmented cost function:</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E9X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{J}_{N}(g,w_{ini},r,\mu)=J_{N}(g,w_{ini},r)+\mu^{T}C^{a}(g)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{J}_{N}(g,w_{ini},r,\mu)=J_{N}(g,w_{ini},r)+\mu^{T}C^{a}(g),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{a}(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{a}(g)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the active constraints at the nominal solution, and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is the Lagrange multiplier vector associated with the active constraints. Let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g^{o},w^{o}_{ini},r^{o})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>o</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g^{o},w^{o}_{ini},r^{o})</annotation></semantics></math> represent nominal solution of DeePC (<a href="#S2.E8" title="In II-B Data-Enabled Predictive Control (DeePC) ‣ II Problem Formulation and Preliminaries ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), which must satisfy the following necessary optimality conditions (KKT conditions):</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{J}_{g}(g,w_{ini},r,\mu)=0,\qquad\mu\geq 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{J}_{g}(g,w_{ini},r,\mu)=0,\qquad\mu\geq 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where the subscript <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> indicates the partial derivative <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial/\partial g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial/\partial g</annotation></semantics></math>; thus, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{J}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{J}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> represents <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\bar{J}_{N}/\partial g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\bar{J}_{N}/\partial g</annotation></semantics></math>, where for simplicity, we show <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{J_{N}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{J_{N}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{J}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{J}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 1</span></span><span id="Thmassumption1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{a}_{g}(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{a}_{g}(g)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmassumption1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, i.e., <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial C^{a}/\partial g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial C^{a}/\partial g</annotation></semantics></math>, is full row rank.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Substituting the nominal solution <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g^{o},w^{o}_{ini},r^{o})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>o</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g^{o},w^{o}_{ini},r^{o})</annotation></semantics></math> into the KKT conditions (<a href="#S3.E10" title="In III-A DeeNE with Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) yields</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E11X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle J_{g}(g^{o},w^{o}_{ini},r^{o})+\mu^{T}C^{a}_{g}(g^{o})=0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>o</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle J_{g}(g^{o},w^{o}_{ini},r^{o})+\mu^{T}C^{a}_{g}(g^{o})=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The Lagrange multiplier can thus be obtained online as:</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E12X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mu=-{(C^{a}_{g}{C^{a}_{g}}^{T})}^{-1}C^{a}_{g}J^{T}_{g}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></mmultiscripts></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mu=-{(C^{a}_{g}{C^{a}_{g}}^{T})}^{-1}C^{a}_{g}J^{T}_{g}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Note that Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ III-A DeeNE with Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> guarantees that <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{a}_{g}{C^{a}_{g}}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{a}_{g}{C^{a}_{g}}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is invertible. Moreover, it is worth noting that <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=0</annotation></semantics></math> if the constraint <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(g^{o})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(g^{o})</annotation></semantics></math> is not active. The Lagrange multiplier (<a href="#S3.E12" title="In III-A DeeNE with Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) is considered as the nominal Lagrange multiplier <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>μ</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{o}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.3" class="ltx_p">Now, consider the nominal solution <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g^{o},u^{o},y^{o},\mu^{o})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g^{o},u^{o},y^{o},\mu^{o})</annotation></semantics></math> under the given <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{o}_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>o</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{o}_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{o}</annotation></semantics></math>. For a new initial I/O trajectory <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{ini}=w^{o}_{ini}+\delta w_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>o</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{ini}=w^{o}_{ini}+\delta w_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math> and a reference trajectory <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=r^{o}+\delta r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=r^{o}+\delta r</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta w_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta w_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta r</annotation></semantics></math> denoting deviations from nominal conditions, the objective is to develop the DeeNE framework which achieves <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta u</annotation></semantics></math> by minimizing the second-order variation of (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In III-A DeeNE with Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) subject to linearized constraints. More specifically, for the given <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta w_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta w_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta r</annotation></semantics></math>, DeeNE solves the following optimization problem:</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E13X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E13X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E13X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{\delta g^{*}}=\underset{\mathbf{\delta g}}{\arg\min}\hskip 2.84526pt{J}^{ne}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐠</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{\delta g^{*}}=\underset{\mathbf{\delta g}}{\arg\min}\hskip 2.84526pt{J}^{ne}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E13Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E13Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s.t.\hskip 14.22636ptC^{a}_{g}\delta g=0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo><mspace style="width:1.587em;" width="1.587em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s.t.\hskip 14.22636ptC^{a}_{g}\delta g=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.4" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E14X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E14X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E14X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\displaystyle J}^{ne}_{N}(\delta g,\delta w_{ini},\delta r)=\delta^{2}\bar{J}_{N}(g^{o},w^{o}_{ini},r^{o})=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>o</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\displaystyle J}^{ne}_{N}(\delta g,\delta w_{ini},\delta r)=\delta^{2}\bar{J}_{N}(g^{o},w^{o}_{ini},r^{o})=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E14Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E14Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix}\delta g\\
\delta w_{ini}\\
\delta r\end{bmatrix}^{T}\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gg}&amp;\bar{J}_{gw_{ini}}&amp;\bar{J}_{gr}\\
\bar{J}_{w_{ini}g}&amp;\bar{J}_{w_{ini}w_{ini}}&amp;\bar{J}_{w_{ini}r}\\
\bar{J}_{rg}&amp;\bar{J}_{rw_{ini}}&amp;\bar{J}_{rr}\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}\delta g\\
\delta w_{ini}\\
\delta r\end{bmatrix}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix}\delta g\\
\delta w_{ini}\\
\delta r\end{bmatrix}^{T}\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gg}&amp;\bar{J}_{gw_{ini}}&amp;\bar{J}_{gr}\\
\bar{J}_{w_{ini}g}&amp;\bar{J}_{w_{ini}w_{ini}}&amp;\bar{J}_{w_{ini}r}\\
\bar{J}_{rg}&amp;\bar{J}_{rw_{ini}}&amp;\bar{J}_{rr}\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}\delta g\\
\delta w_{ini}\\
\delta r\end{bmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.5" class="ltx_p">where the subscripts <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="gg" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">gg</annotation></semantics></math> and others represent the second-order partial derivatives.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">For (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In III-A DeeNE with Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>), the augmented cost function is obtained as</p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E15X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E15X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E15X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{J}^{ne}_{N}(\delta g,\delta w_{ini},\delta r,\delta\mu)={J}^{ne}_{N}(\delta g,\delta w_{ini},\delta r)+\delta\mu^{T}C^{a}_{g}(g)\delta g," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{J}^{ne}_{N}(\delta g,\delta w_{ini},\delta r,\delta\mu)={J}^{ne}_{N}(\delta g,\delta w_{ini},\delta r)+\delta\mu^{T}C^{a}_{g}(g)\delta g,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is the Lagrange multiplier of the optimization problem (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In III-A DeeNE with Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>). Applying the KKT conditions to (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In III-A DeeNE with Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), one has</p>
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{J}^{ne}_{\delta g}=0,\qquad\delta\mu\geq 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{J}^{ne}_{\delta g}=0,\qquad\delta\mu\geq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{J}^{ne}_{\delta g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{J}^{ne}_{\delta g}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\bar{J}^{ne}_{N}/\partial\delta g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\bar{J}^{ne}_{N}/\partial\delta g</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal)<span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider the optimization problem (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In III-A DeeNE with Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>), the augmented cost function (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In III-A DeeNE with Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), and the KKT conditions (<a href="#S3.E16" title="In III-A DeeNE with Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>). If <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{J}_{gg}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{J}_{gg}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the DeeNE policy</span></p>
<table id="S3.E17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E17X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E17X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E17X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\delta g=K^{*}_{1}\delta w_{ini}+K^{*}_{2}\delta r," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\delta g=K^{*}_{1}\delta w_{ini}+K^{*}_{2}\delta r,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="4" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E17Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E17Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle K^{*}_{1}=-\begin{bmatrix}I&amp;0\end{bmatrix}K^{o}\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gw_{ini}}\\
{0}\end{bmatrix}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>I</mi></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle K^{*}_{1}=-\begin{bmatrix}I&amp;0\end{bmatrix}K^{o}\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gw_{ini}}\\
{0}\end{bmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E17Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E17Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle K^{*}_{2}=-\begin{bmatrix}I&amp;0\end{bmatrix}K^{o}\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gr}\\
0\end{bmatrix}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>I</mi></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle K^{*}_{2}=-\begin{bmatrix}I&amp;0\end{bmatrix}K^{o}\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gr}\\
0\end{bmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E17Xc" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E17Xc.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle K^{o}=\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gg}&{C^{a}_{g}}^{T}\\
C^{a}_{g}&amp;0\end{bmatrix}^{-1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mmultiscripts><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></mmultiscripts></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle K^{o}=\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gg}&amp;{C^{a}_{g}}^{T}\\
C^{a}_{g}&amp;0\end{bmatrix}^{-1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">approximates the perturbed solution for the DeePC (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In II-B Data-Enabled Predictive Control (DeePC) ‣ II Problem Formulation and Preliminaries ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) in the presence of initial I/O perturbation <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta w_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta w_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math> and reference perturbation <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta r</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p7.4.1" class="ltx_text">Using (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In III-A DeeNE with Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) and the KKT conditions (<a href="#S3.E16" title="In III-A DeeNE with Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>), one has</span></p>
<table id="S3.E18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E18X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E18X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E18X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{J}_{gg}\delta g+\bar{J}_{gw_{ini}}\delta w_{ini}+\bar{J}_{gr}\delta r+{C^{a}_{g}}^{T}\delta\mu=0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{J}_{gg}\delta g+\bar{J}_{gw_{ini}}\delta w_{ini}+\bar{J}_{gr}\delta r+{C^{a}_{g}}^{T}\delta\mu=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p7.5.1" class="ltx_text">Now, using (<a href="#S3.E18" title="In Proof. ‣ III-A DeeNE with Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>) and the linearized system constraints (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In III-A DeeNE with Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>), one has</span></p>
<table id="S3.E19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E19X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E19X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E19X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gg}&{C^{a}_{g}}^{T}\\
C^{a}_{g}&amp;0\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}\delta g\\
\delta\mu\end{bmatrix}=-\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gw_{ini}}\\
0\end{bmatrix}\delta w_{ini}-\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gr}\\
0\end{bmatrix}\delta r," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mmultiscripts><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></mmultiscripts></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gg}&amp;{C^{a}_{g}}^{T}\\
C^{a}_{g}&amp;0\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}\delta g\\
\delta\mu\end{bmatrix}=-\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gw_{ini}}\\
0\end{bmatrix}\delta w_{ini}-\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gr}\\
0\end{bmatrix}\delta r,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p7.6.1" class="ltx_text">which yields</span></p>
<table id="S3.E20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E20X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E20X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E20X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{bmatrix}\delta g\\
\delta\mu\end{bmatrix}=-K^{o}\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gw_{ini}}\\
0\end{bmatrix}\delta w_{ini}-K^{o}\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gr}\\
0\end{bmatrix}\delta r." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{bmatrix}\delta g\\
\delta\mu\end{bmatrix}=-K^{o}\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gw_{ini}}\\
0\end{bmatrix}\delta w_{ini}-K^{o}\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gr}\\
0\end{bmatrix}\delta r.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p7.7.1" class="ltx_text">Thus, the DeeNE policy (<a href="#S3.E17" title="In Theorem 1 (Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal). ‣ III-A DeeNE with Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>) is obtained, and the proof is completed.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1</span></span><span id="Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Singularity)<span id="Thmremark1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The assumption of <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{J}_{gg}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{J}_{gg}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ III-A DeeNE with Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> are essential for DeeNE since the first one guarantees the convexity of (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In III-A DeeNE with Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>), and both guarantee a non-singular <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{o}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E17" title="In Theorem 1 (Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal). ‣ III-A DeeNE with Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>). If <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{a}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{a}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> was not full row rank, the matrix <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{o}</annotation></semantics></math> would be singular, which leads to the failure of the proposed algorithm. This can be addressed using the constraint back-propagation algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2</span></span><span id="Thmremark2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Using the control policy (<a href="#S3.E17" title="In Theorem 1 (Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal). ‣ III-A DeeNE with Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>), one can obtain <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}=g^{o}+\delta g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}=g^{o}+\delta g</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{*}=u^{o}+\delta u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{*}=u^{o}+\delta u</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained using <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=U_{F}g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=U_{F}g</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, one can conclude that <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta u=K_{ne}^{r}\delta r+K_{ne}^{ini}\delta w_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta u=K_{ne}^{r}\delta r+K_{ne}^{ini}\delta w_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">DeeNE with Non-Optimal Nominal Solution</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In the previous part, DeeNE was derived under the assumption of an available nominal DeePC solution; thus, the nominal solution is optimal. In this subsection, we tackle the DeeNE policy for a nominal non-optimal solution so that at each time step, we can use the previous DeeNE solution as the nominal solution in our algorithm. For a nominal non-optimal solution <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g^{o},w^{o}_{ini},r^{o})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>o</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g^{o},w^{o}_{ini},r^{o})</annotation></semantics></math>, we assume that it satisfies the constraints described in (<a href="#S2.E8" title="In II-B Data-Enabled Predictive Control (DeePC) ‣ II Problem Formulation and Preliminaries ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) but may not satisfy the optimality condition <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{J}_{g}(g^{o},w_{ini}^{o},r^{o},\mu^{o})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>o</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{J}_{g}(g^{o},w_{ini}^{o},r^{o},\mu^{o})=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Under this circumstance, the cost function (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In III-A DeeNE with Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) is modified for DeeNE as:</p>
<table id="S3.E21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E21X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E21X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E21X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\displaystyle J}^{ne}_{N}(\delta g,\delta w_{ini},\delta r)=\delta^{2}\bar{J}_{N}(g^{o},w^{o}_{ini},r^{o})+\delta\bar{J}_{N}(g^{o},w^{o}_{ini},r^{o})=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">J</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">N</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">g</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">r</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">δ</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">g</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">o</mi></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">o</mi></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">r</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">o</mi></msup><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">g</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">o</mi></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">o</mi></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">r</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">o</mi></msup><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\displaystyle J}^{ne}_{N}(\delta g,\delta w_{ini},\delta r)=\delta^{2}\bar{J}_{N}(g^{o},w^{o}_{ini},r^{o})+\delta\bar{J}_{N}(g^{o},w^{o}_{ini},r^{o})=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E21Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E21Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix}\delta g\\
\delta w_{ini}\\
\delta r\end{bmatrix}^{T}\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gg}&amp;\bar{J}_{gw_{ini}}&amp;\bar{J}_{gr}\\
\bar{J}_{w_{ini}g}&amp;\bar{J}_{w_{ini}w_{ini}}&amp;\bar{J}_{w_{ini}r}\\
\bar{J}_{rg}&amp;\bar{J}_{rw_{ini}}&amp;\bar{J}_{rr}\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}\delta g\\
\delta w_{ini}\\
\delta r\end{bmatrix}+\bar{J}^{T}_{g}\delta g." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">g</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">r</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">g</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">r</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">g</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">r</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">g</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">r</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">g</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">r</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">g</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix}\delta g\\
\delta w_{ini}\\
\delta r\end{bmatrix}^{T}\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gg}&amp;\bar{J}_{gw_{ini}}&amp;\bar{J}_{gr}\\
\bar{J}_{w_{ini}g}&amp;\bar{J}_{w_{ini}w_{ini}}&amp;\bar{J}_{w_{ini}r}\\
\bar{J}_{rg}&amp;\bar{J}_{rw_{ini}}&amp;\bar{J}_{rr}\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}\delta g\\
\delta w_{ini}\\
\delta r\end{bmatrix}+\bar{J}^{T}_{g}\delta g.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Considering the optimal control problem (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In III-A DeeNE with Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) and the cost function (<a href="#S3.E21" title="In III-B DeeNE with Non-Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>), the augmented cost function is modified as:</p>
<table id="S3.E22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E22X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E22X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E22X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{J}^{ne}_{N}(\delta g,\delta w_{ini},\delta r,\delta\mu)=\delta^{2}\bar{J}_{N}(g^{o},w^{o}_{ini},r^{o})+\delta\mu^{T}C^{a}_{g}(g)\delta g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>o</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{J}^{ne}_{N}(\delta g,\delta w_{ini},\delta r,\delta\mu)=\delta^{2}\bar{J}_{N}(g^{o},w^{o}_{ini},r^{o})+\delta\mu^{T}C^{a}_{g}(g)\delta g</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E22Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E22Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\bar{J}^{T}_{g}(g^{o},w^{o}_{ini},r^{o})\delta g." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>o</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\bar{J}^{T}_{g}(g^{o},w^{o}_{ini},r^{o})\delta g.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Now, the following theorem is presented to modify the DeeNE policy for the nominal non-optimal solutions.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Modified Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal)<span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider the optimization problem (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In III-A DeeNE with Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>), the KKT conditions (<a href="#S3.E16" title="In III-A DeeNE with Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>), and the augmented cost function (<a href="#S3.E22" title="In III-B DeeNE with Non-Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>). If <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{J}_{gg}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{J}_{gg}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, then the DeeNE policy is modified for a nominal non-optimal solution as</span></p>
<table id="S3.E23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E23X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E23X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E23X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\delta g=K^{*}_{1}\delta w_{ini}+K^{*}_{2}\delta r+K^{*}_{3}\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{g}\\
{0}\end{bmatrix}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\delta g=K^{*}_{1}\delta w_{ini}+K^{*}_{2}\delta r+K^{*}_{3}\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{g}\\
{0}\end{bmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E23Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E23Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle K^{*}_{3}=-\begin{bmatrix}I&amp;0\end{bmatrix}K^{o}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>I</mi></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>o</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle K^{*}_{3}=-\begin{bmatrix}I&amp;0\end{bmatrix}K^{o},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the gain matrices <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{*}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{*}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{*}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{*}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{o}</annotation></semantics></math> are defined in (<a href="#S3.E17" title="In Theorem 1 (Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal). ‣ III-A DeeNE with Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.4.1" class="ltx_text">Using the KKT conditions (<a href="#S3.E16" title="In III-A DeeNE with Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) and the modified augmented cost function (<a href="#S3.E22" title="In III-B DeeNE with Non-Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>), one has</span></p>
<table id="S3.E24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E24X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E24X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E24X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{J}_{gg}\delta g+\bar{J}_{gw_{ini}}\delta w_{ini}+\bar{J}_{gr}\delta r+{C^{a}_{g}}^{T}\delta\mu+\bar{J}_{g}=0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{J}_{gg}\delta g+\bar{J}_{gw_{ini}}\delta w_{ini}+\bar{J}_{gr}\delta r+{C^{a}_{g}}^{T}\delta\mu+\bar{J}_{g}=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.5.1" class="ltx_text">Now, using (<a href="#S3.E24" title="In Proof. ‣ III-B DeeNE with Non-Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>) and the linearized system constraints (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In III-A DeeNE with Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>), one has</span></p>
<table id="S3.E25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E25X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E25X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E25X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gg}&{C^{a}_{g}}^{T}\\
C^{a}_{g}&amp;0\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}\delta g\\
\delta\mu\end{bmatrix}=-\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gw_{ini}}\\
0\end{bmatrix}\delta w_{ini}-\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gr}\\
0\end{bmatrix}\delta r-\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{g}\\
{0}\end{bmatrix}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mmultiscripts><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></mmultiscripts></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gg}&amp;{C^{a}_{g}}^{T}\\
C^{a}_{g}&amp;0\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}\delta g\\
\delta\mu\end{bmatrix}=-\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gw_{ini}}\\
0\end{bmatrix}\delta w_{ini}-\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gr}\\
0\end{bmatrix}\delta r-\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{g}\\
{0}\end{bmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.6.1" class="ltx_text">which yields</span></p>
<table id="S3.E26" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E26X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E26X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E26X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{bmatrix}\delta g\\
\delta\mu\end{bmatrix}=-K^{o}\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gw_{ini}}\\
0\end{bmatrix}\delta w_{ini}-K^{o}\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gr}\\
0\end{bmatrix}\delta r-K^{o}\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{g}\\
{0}\end{bmatrix}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{bmatrix}\delta g\\
\delta\mu\end{bmatrix}=-K^{o}\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gw_{ini}}\\
0\end{bmatrix}\delta w_{ini}-K^{o}\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gr}\\
0\end{bmatrix}\delta r-K^{o}\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{g}\\
{0}\end{bmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.7.1" class="ltx_text">Thus, the modified DeeNE policy (<a href="#S3.E23" title="In Theorem 2 (Modified Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal). ‣ III-B DeeNE with Non-Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>) is obtained, and the proof is completed.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3</span></span><span id="Thmremark3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Quadratic Cost)<span id="Thmremark3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark3.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">One can consider a quadratic cost function <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{N}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{g},\mathbf{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐮</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{N}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{g},\mathbf{r})</annotation></semantics></math> as:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E27" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E27X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E27X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E27X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle J_{N}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{g},\mathbf{r})={\|y-r\|}_{Q}^{2}+{\|u\|}_{R}^{2}+\lambda_{y}{\|\sigma_{y}\|}_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐮</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi>𝐠</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle J_{N}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{g},\mathbf{r})={\|y-r\|}_{Q}^{2}+{\|u\|}_{R}^{2}+\lambda_{y}{\|\sigma_{y}\|}_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E27Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E27Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\lambda_{u}{\|\sigma_{u}\|}_{2}^{2}+\lambda_{g}{\|g\|}_{2}^{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\lambda_{u}{\|\sigma_{u}\|}_{2}^{2}+\lambda_{g}{\|g\|}_{2}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmremark3.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark3.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the positive semi-definite matrix <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\in\mathbb{R}^{pN\times pN}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\in\mathbb{R}^{pN\times pN}</annotation></semantics></math> and the positive definite matrix <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\in\mathbb{R}^{mN\times mN}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\in\mathbb{R}^{mN\times mN}</annotation></semantics></math> are weighting matrices, and the positive parameters <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{y},\lambda_{u},\lambda_{g}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{y},\lambda_{u},\lambda_{g}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> are regularization weights. For the quadratic cost function (<a href="#S3.E27" title="In Remark 3 (Quadratic Cost). ‣ III-B DeeNE with Non-Optimal Nominal Solution ‣ III Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal (DeeNE) ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>), the DeePC is a quadratic programming (QP) problem on the decision variable <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, which requires an iterative QP solver. However, using DeeNE, one can have a computationally efficient solution to this optimization problem without requiring an iterative solver. For DeeNE framework, one has</span></p>
<table id="S3.E28" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E28X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E28X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E28X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{J}_{g}=2((Y_{F}g-r)^{T}QY_{F}+(U_{F}g)^{T}RU_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{J}_{g}=2((Y_{F}g-r)^{T}QY_{F}+(U_{F}g)^{T}RU_{F}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="5" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E28Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E28Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\lambda_{y}(Y_{P}g-y_{ini})^{T}Y_{P}+\lambda_{u}(U_{P}g-u_{ini})^{T}U_{P}+\lambda_{g}g^{T})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\lambda_{y}(Y_{P}g-y_{ini})^{T}Y_{P}+\lambda_{u}(U_{P}g-u_{ini})^{T}U_{P}+\lambda_{g}g^{T}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E28Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E28Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{J}_{gg}=2(Y^{T}_{F}QY_{F}+U^{T}_{F}RU_{F}+\lambda_{y}Y^{T}_{P}Y_{P}+\lambda_{u}U^{T}_{P}U_{P}+\lambda_{g})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>P</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>P</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{J}_{gg}=2(Y^{T}_{F}QY_{F}+U^{T}_{F}RU_{F}+\lambda_{y}Y^{T}_{P}Y_{P}+\lambda_{u}U^{T}_{P}U_{P}+\lambda_{g}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E28Xc" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E28Xc.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{J}_{gw_{ini}}=-2(\lambda_{y}Y^{T}_{P}+\lambda_{u}U^{T}_{P})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>P</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>P</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{J}_{gw_{ini}}=-2(\lambda_{y}Y^{T}_{P}+\lambda_{u}U^{T}_{P}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E28Xd" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E28Xd.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{J}_{gr}=-2Y^{T}_{F}Q," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{J}_{gr}=-2Y^{T}_{F}Q,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmremark3.p1.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark3.p1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the requirement <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{J}_{gg}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{J}_{gg}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is satisfied.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.4" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">DeeNE Implementation</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this subsection, we present how DeeNE is implemented for efficient control of autonomous systems, which is summarized in Algorithm 1. Given the pre-collected Hankel matrices, an initial I/O trajectory <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math> and the reference trajectory <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, it uses DeePC (<a href="#S2.E8" title="In II-B Data-Enabled Predictive Control (DeePC) ‣ II Problem Formulation and Preliminaries ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) for the time step <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=T_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=T_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math> to generate an N-length solution. Then, the DeeNE framework applies the first <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> control inputs <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{*}(k:k+s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{*}(k:k+s)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s\in[0:1:N]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in[0:1:N]</annotation></semantics></math> is a hyper-parameter that can be tuned. For the remaining <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{c}-s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{c}-s</annotation></semantics></math> steps, where <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is the simulation/experiment time, the optimal adaptation laws developed above are used to correct the control action through <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta g</annotation></semantics></math>, based on the deviations <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta w_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta w_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta r</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that at the first correction step, i.e., <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=T_{ini}+s+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=T_{ini}+s+1</annotation></semantics></math>, Theorem 1 and Theorem 2 represent the same control policy having <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{J}_{g}(g^{o},w_{ini}^{o},r^{o},\mu^{o})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>o</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{J}_{g}(g^{o},w_{ini}^{o},r^{o},\mu^{o})=0</annotation></semantics></math> since we have a nominal DeePC solution. However, Theorem 2 needs to be used for the remaining steps to consider the nominal solution as the DeeNE solution from the previous step. This process then continues for the next steps. In particular, only one DeePC optimization is performed throughout the simulation/experiment time <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, significantly reducing computational complexity compared to the traditional DeePC which requires solving a new optimization problem  (<a href="#S2.E8" title="In II-B Data-Enabled Predictive Control (DeePC) ‣ II Problem Formulation and Preliminaries ‣ Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal: Case Study on Model-Free Trajectory Tracking for Robotic Arm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) with updated <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> at each step.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.SS3.fig1" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="S3.SS3.fig1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:293.3pt;height:479.9pt;vertical-align:-474.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-25.9pt,42.3pt) scale(0.8,0.8) ;">
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span>Modified DeeNE</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.3" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.3.1" class="ltx_listingline">
<span id="alg1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parameter :</span> <math id="alg1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
</div>
<div id="alg1.3.2" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="alg1.3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input :</span> <math id="alg1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{ini}(0:T_{ini}-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{ini}(0:T_{ini}-1)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(0:N-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(0:N-1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
</div>
<div id="alg1.3.3" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="alg1.3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output :</span> <math id="alg1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}(0:T_{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}(0:T_{c})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}(0:T_{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}(0:T_{c})</annotation></semantics></math>.
</div>
<div id="alg1.3.4" class="ltx_listingline"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:1px;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.3.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1</span></span>
                <span id="alg1.3.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <em id="alg1.3.4.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="alg1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=T_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=T_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math> : s : <math id="alg1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{c}</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="alg1.3.4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
